 ## Proof
 
 **Proof technique:** direct; differentiate the retarded potentials, use conservation to remove the longitudinal scalar-potential term, estimate each geometric and time-shift remainder uniformly, and pass the resulting uniform flux limit through the finite sphere integral.
 
 1.1 Geometry of the observation point. Fix $n\in S^2$, $u\in K$, $r\ge2a$, and $y\in B_a$. Put $\sigma=n\cdot y$, $R=|rn-y|$, $N=(rn-y)/R$, $v=u+\sigma/c$, and $\delta=(r-\sigma-R)/c$, so $u+r/c-R/c=v+\delta$. Since $r-\sigma\ge r-a\ge r/2$, the identity $R^2=(r-\sigma)^2+|y|^2-\sigma^2$ gives $|R-(r-\sigma)|=(|y|^2-\sigma^2)/(R+r-\sigma)\le a^2/r$; hence $|\delta|\le a^2/(rc)\le a/(2c)$. Also $|r-R|\le|\sigma|+a^2/r\le3a/2$, so $R\ge r/2$, $|N-n|\le|(r-R)n-y|/R\le5a/r$, $|R^{-1}-r^{-1}|=|r-R|/(rR)\le3a/r^2$, and $|N/R-n/r|\le|N-n|/R+|R^{-1}-r^{-1}|\le13a/r^2$. Thus all retarded source arguments and the intervals between them lie in $K^\sharp$. [given, F5, algebra, A1]
 
-1.2 Retarded-field integrals. The set form of [F4] requires a compact measurable integration set of finite measure, so use $\overline B_a$, which has these properties by [F7]. Since $\rho,J$ are jointly smooth and vanish for $|y|>a$, their values and their time derivatives vanish on $\partial B_a$; hence the potential integrals and the integrals obtained by one $x$- or $t$-derivative have the same values over $B_a$ and $\overline B_a$. Fix an observation event with $|x|>a$ and a product neighborhood with compact closure in the exterior region $|x|>a$. For each observation coordinate $x_i$ and $t$, hold the other coordinates fixed and apply the set form of [F4] on $\overline B_a$ with that coordinate as parameter. The retarded integrands and their parameter derivatives are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times the chosen parameter interval, since $R=|x-y|>0$ there. Apply this argument to $\rho$ and separately to each component of $J$, for each spatial-coordinate and time derivative needed in $\nabla\varphi$, $\partial_tA$ and $\operatorname{curl}A$. The chain rule and algebra of derivatives give $\nabla_xR=N$, $\nabla_x(t-R/c)=-N/c$, and $\nabla_x(R^{-1})=-N/R^2$. Substitution in $E=-\nabla\varphi-\partial_tA$ and $B=\operatorname{curl}A$ therefore gives, at $x=rn$, $t=u+r/c$, $\displaystyle E=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int_{B_a}\left(\frac{c^2N\rho_{\mathrm{ret}}}{R^2}+\frac{cN\rho_{t,\mathrm{ret}}}{R}-\frac{J_{t,\mathrm{ret}}}{R}\right)dy,\qquad B=-\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int_{B_a}N\times\left(\frac{J_{\mathrm{ret}}}{R^2}+\frac{J_{t,\mathrm{ret}}}{cR}\right)dy,$ where the subscript “ret” means evaluation at $v+\delta$. Here $R\ge r-a>0$ for $r\ge2a$. [F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, algebra, A1]
