-1.3 Differentiation of the retarded current moment. Choose a bounded open interval $U$ containing $K$ and a compact interval $K_0$ whose interior contains $U$. Since $J$ and $J_t$ vanish on $\partial B_a$ by the smooth spatial-support hypothesis, the integral over $B_a$ agrees with that over the compact measurable finite-measure set $\overline B_a$ [F7]. For fixed $n\in S^2$, the map $(y,u)\mapsto J(y,u+n\cdot y/c)$ and its $u$ derivative $J_t(y,u+n\cdot y/c)$ are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a\times K_0$. Apply the set form of [F4] separately to each of the three vector components with integration set $\overline B_a$ and parameter $u$. Thus $\partial_u I(n,u)=\int_{B_a}J_t(y,u+n\cdot y/c)\,dy$ for every $u\in U$, so the definition of $V$ in the Statement is well-defined on a neighborhood of $K$. [given, F1, F4, F7, algebra, A1]
